Inferno copy of fdlibm 5.3, ported to SoftFloat
================================================

The sources are those of the Sun/netlib fdlibm 5.3 distribution (the upstream
README is kept as README), reduced to what Inferno needs and ported to
SoftFloat, so that elementary functions give the same bits on every machine,
whatever its floating-point hardware.

Reduction
---------

The build system (Makefile*, configure*), the SVID/X-Open error wrappers
(w_*.c, k_standard.c, s_matherr.c, s_lib_version.c, s_signgam.c) and the
functions Inferno does not use (gamma, scalb, significand, logb, frexp, ldexp,
isnan, lgamma without _r) were removed: 44 sources remain.  Errors are
reported through IEEE exceptions only.

Names
-----

Every external symbol carries the fd_ prefix, so that it cannot clash with the
host's libm:

	__ieee754_NAME   ->  fd_NAME           (fd_exp, fd_pow, fd_lgamma_r ...)
	__kernel_NAME    ->  fd_kernel_NAME    (fd_kernel_sin ...)
	NAME             ->  fd_NAME           (fd_sin, fd_floor, fd_modf ...)

The calls that the original code made to the public sqrt() go to fd_sqrt(),
fdlibm's own square root.

The port
--------

The algorithms are those of fdlibm 5.3 and computation is done on float64_t,
the type of SoftFloat, instead of the `double' of the host.  Nothing depends
on the host's floating-point unit, its byte order or its C library any more.

  - the operators of C are the operations of <inferno/fp.h>, under the short
    names of fdlibm.h: FADD FSUB FMUL FDIV FNEG, the comparisons FEQ FNE FLT
    FLE FGT FGE, the conversions FI FU FTOI.  Each operation rounds as the
    operator did on a machine with strict IEEE 754 binary64 arithmetic;

  - the two words of a number, which the original reached with __HI and __LO
    (and a probe of the byte order of the host in e_pow, e_cosh and e_sinh),
    are read with FHI and FLO and written with FSETHI and FSETLO;

  - integers are int32_t and uint32_t;

  - constants are declared with F64K and F64KA.  The markers define nothing for
    the compiler: utils/fdconst reads the source and generates, at build time,
    a header (file.k.h) that defines the constants from their decimal text,
    with integer arithmetic only.  A constant can be edited in decimal;

  - every statement that was rewritten keeps its original text in a comment.

fdlibm only accumulates the exception flags of SoftFloat: it does not trap and
does not choose the rounding mode.  Like the original it is written for round
to nearest; whoever calls it sets the mode, clears the flags before and reads
them after.

Tests
-----

../test/oracle.c compares every function with ../test/ref, the same sources
before the port, computed by the host with strict IEEE 754 arithmetic: bit for
bit, with operands built from the thresholds of the algorithms.  Run mk test
in ../test.

Status
------

The directory is not built into libmath.a yet: libmath/mkfile still builds
fdlibm-old.  Switching comes after the tests of the new fdlibm and SoftFloat
are satisfactory.
